## Solution

We compute the limit of the regular distribution $T_{\frac{\sin(\lambda x)}{x}}$ in $\mathcal{D}'(\mathbb{R})$ as $\lambda\to\infty$. By definition, for $\varphi\in\mathcal{D}(\mathbb{R})$ (smooth, compactly supported),

$$\langle T_{\frac{\sin(\lambda x)}{x}},\varphi\rangle = \int_{\mathbb{R}}\frac{\sin(\lambda x)}{x}\,\varphi(x)\,dx,$$

where the integrand vanishes outside $\operatorname{supp}\varphi$, and the singularity at $x=0$ is removable since $\frac{\sin(\lambda x)}{x}\to\lambda$ as $x\to 0$.

**Claim.** $\displaystyle\lim_{\lambda\to\infty}T_{\frac{\sin(\lambda x)}{x}}=\pi\,\delta_0$ in $\mathcal{D}'(\mathbb{R})$.

**Proof.** Fix $\varphi\in\mathcal{D}(\mathbb{R})$ and choose $\psi\in\mathcal{D}(\mathbb{R})$ with $\psi\equiv 1$ on a neighborhood of $0$. Decompose

$$\varphi(x)=\varphi(0)\,\psi(x)+\bigl(\varphi(x)-\varphi(0)\,\psi(x)\bigr).$$

Set $g(x):=\frac{\varphi(x)-\varphi(0)\,\psi(x)}{x}$. Near $0$, since $\psi\equiv 1$, we have $g(x)=\frac{\varphi(x)-\varphi(0)}{x}\to\varphi'(0)$, so $g$ extends smoothly across $0$; away from $0$ it is $\varphi(x)/x$, smooth and compactly supported. Hence $g\in\mathcal{D}(\mathbb{R})\subset L^{1}(\mathbb{R})$. By the **Riemann–Lebesgue lemma**,

$$\int_{\mathbb{R}}\sin(\lambda x)\,g(x)\,dx \;\xrightarrow[\lambda\to\infty]{}\; 0.$$

It remains to evaluate

$$J_\lambda:=\int_{\mathbb{R}}\frac{\sin(\lambda x)}{x}\,\psi(x)\,dx.$$

Substituting $u=\lambda x$,

$$J_\lambda=\int_{\mathbb{R}}\frac{\sin u}{u}\,\psi\!\left(\tfrac{u}{\lambda}\right)du=\int_{-\lambda R}^{\lambda R}\frac{\sin u}{u}\,\psi\!\left(\tfrac{u}{\lambda}\right)du,$$

where $\operatorname{supp}\psi\subseteq[-R,R]$. We show $J_\lambda\to\pi$, using $\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\frac{\sin u}{u}\,du=\pi$.

Let $\varepsilon>0$. Choose $M>0$ so that $\left|\int_{M}^{A}\frac{\sin u}{u}\,du\right|<\varepsilon$ and $\left|\int_{-A}^{-M}\frac{\sin u}{u}\,du\right|<\varepsilon$ for all $A\ge M$ (possible since the improper integral converges). On the compact interval $[-M,M]$, $\psi(u/\lambda)\to\psi(0)=1$ uniformly, so for $\lambda$ large,

$$\left|\int_{-M}^{M}\frac{\sin u}{u}\bigl(\psi(u/\lambda)-1\bigr)\,du\right|<\varepsilon.$$

For the tails $M\le|u|\le\lambda R$, use $|\psi|\le 1$ and the Dirichlet estimate: since $\frac{1}{u}$ is decreasing to $0$ and $\sin u$ has bounded primitive, $\left|\int_{M}^{\lambda R}\frac{\sin u}{u}\,du\right|\le\frac{C}{M}$, and similarly for the negative tail; choosing $M$ large makes each tail $<\varepsilon$ uniformly in $\lambda$. Combining,

$$|J_\lambda-\pi|\le\varepsilon+\varepsilon+\varepsilon+\varepsilon\quad\text{for }\lambda\text{ large},$$

so $J_\lambda\to\pi$.

Putting everything together,

$$\langle T_{\frac{\sin(\lambda x)}{x}},\varphi\rangle = \varphi(0)\,J_\lambda + \int\sin(\lambda x)\,g(x)\,dx \;\xrightarrow[\lambda\to\infty]{}\; \varphi(0)\cdot\pi + 0 = \pi\,\varphi(0)=\langle\pi\,\delta_0,\varphi\rangle.$$

Since this holds for every test function $\varphi$,

$$\boxed{\lim_{\lambda\to\infty}T_{\frac{\sin(\lambda x)}{x}}=\pi\,\delta_0\quad\text{in }\mathcal{D}'(\mathbb{R}).}$$

